----+----+----+----+\n     a    b    c    d    e    f    g    h \nYour valid moves are (src-dst-promotion, or src-dst):\n[R: a1-b1, P: a2-a4, P: a2-a3, P: b2-b4, P: b2-b3, B: c1-d2, B: c1-e3, B: c1-f4, B: c1-g5, B: c1-h6, N: c3-a4, N: c3-b1, N: c3-b5, N: c3-e2, Q: d1-d2, Q: d1-d3, Q: d1-d4, Q: d1-e2, Q: d1-f3, Q: d1-g4, P: d5-e6, P: d5-d6, K: e1-d2, K: e1-e2, P: e4-e5, B: f1-e2, B: f1-d3, B: f1-c4, B: f1-b5, B: f1-a6, P: f2-f4, P: f2-f3, P: g2-g3, R: h1-g1, P: h2-h4, P: h2-h3]\nYour answer format should be: Answer: piece-name: move. \nPiece-name and move (src-dst-promotion or src-dst) should be directly copied EXACTLY from valid move list.\nExample of valid output format: N: a1-b3; P: e7-e8-q; Piece B: g5-f4.\nPlease select a move from your valid moves that maximize your probability of winning.\n', 'reasoning': '', 'output': "Evaluation:  \nSeveral moves are promising in this position. The pawn push P: d5-e6 stands out as it attacks the black knight on f7 (which is undefended) and opens a strong diagonal for the light-squared bishop on c1. Capturing on e6 also prepares potential development or central control. Alternatively, P: e4-e5 could gain space in the center and restrict Black’s pieces, but e5 might be met with easy counterplay. Another candidate is B: c1-g5, pinning the knight on f6 if it were there, but currently that knight is gone, so the bishop has limited immediate impact. N: c3-e2 or N: c3-b5 may reposition the knight, but b5 is less effective due to lack of targets. The move P: d5-e6 creates an immediate threat and improves piece activity.\n\nThe pawn on d5 is supported by the white king and can advance safely. After e6, if Black responds poorly, White can follow up with B: c1-h6 or Q: d1-d8+ or even N: c3-d5 depending on responses. This aggressive central push increases pressure on Black's position and limits counterplay.\n\nReason:  \nP: d5-e6 is tactically strong, attacking the knight on f7 (if Black had not already moved it — but in this case, Nf6 is gone), however, even without capturing a piece, advancing to e6 gains valuable space, supports future development, and potentially weakens Black’s kingside structure. Additionally, it enables rapid kingside coordination and avoids passivity. While P: e4-e5 is also good, e6 leads to more immediate threats and better diagonals for minor pieces.\n\nAnswer: P: d5-e6"}
